<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>critical-thinking</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>critical-thinking</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Critical Thinking</li><li>Literary Analysis and Interpretation</li></ul><h2>Critical Thinking</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Critical Thinking — Critical Thinking, English (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: critical thinking · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Critical Thinking</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Critical Thinking&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: critical thinking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Critical Thinking (Critical Thinking, English, class-11-notes) covers Critical Thinking. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Literary Analysis and Interpretation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Literary Analysis and Interpretation — Critical Thinking, English (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: literary analysis, interpretation · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Interpretation.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Literary Analysis</td></tr><tr><td>• Interpretation</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Literary Analysis&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: literary analysis, interpretation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Literary Analysis and Interpretation (Critical Thinking, English, class-11-notes) covers Literary Analysis, Interpretation. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Critical Thinking</td><td>Critical Thinking (Critical Thinking, English, class-11-notes) covers Critical Thinking. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Literary Analysis and Interpretation</td><td>Literary Analysis and Interpretation (Critical Thinking, English, class-11-notes) covers Literary Analysis, Interpretation. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topi</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Critical Thinking.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Close reading builds an interpretation from precise details—word choice, syntax, imagery, sound, form and structure. A label such as ‘metaphor’ or ‘alliteration’ is only a starting observation; explain its effect in this particular passage.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Support claims with accurate, located evidence and distinguish observation from interpretation. When two readings fit, explain evidence for each and acknowledge counterevidence; do not state authorial intention as fact without a source.</p><h3>Open research question — How evidence supports more than one reading</h3><p>Literary interpretation is evidence-based rather than a hunt for one hidden answer. A defensible open reading offers at least two text-supported interpretations, cites exact details, and explains what complicates each; authorial intention should not be asserted without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>In “Critical Thinking”, which two interpretations can the exact wording support, and what detail complicates each? Record the line or passage; leave the issue open if the text does not decide it.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.york.ac.uk/english/about/writing-at-york/writing-resources/close-reading/">University of York: close reading and ambiguity</a></li><li><a href="https://writing.wisc.edu/handbook/academicwriting/closereading/">UW–Madison: close reading</a></li><li><a href="https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/general_writing/academic_writing/rhetorical_situation/elements_of_rhetorical_situations.html">Purdue OWL: rhetorical situation</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>